From f3a58c27110a9e1d7e982d4069352d47baf9b7b0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 22:49:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 12/15] PSCTRL: the PISTORM_* tunables become live globals

Twenty-odd knobs that were read once with getenv() are now plain aligned
ints in platforms/atari/psctrl/psctrl_tunables.c, re-read where they are
used. That is what lets the settings accessory change them on a running
machine: a single 32-bit store is atomic on AArch64, so no reader needs a
lock, no syscall happens, and nothing is added to core 3's admission
budget - those loops already read globals.

Precedence is unchanged in practice and now written down: built-in
default, then the .cfg key, then the environment. psctrl_tunables_init()
runs after the config load and only overrides what is actually set in the
environment, so a PISTORM_* in an existing launch script still wins.

Three behaviour changes, all deliberate:

* One truth rule for the boolean flags. The tree had three - `== '1'`,
  `non-empty && != "0"`, and bare presence - so PISTORM_ACSI_DEBUG=2
  enabled the trace in acsi.c but not the one in atari_fdd.c, and
  PISTORM_MFP_DEBUG=0 silenced mfp_trace() while leaving the REZ trace in
  pistorm_natmem.cpp printing. Set is on, "0" is off, a number is that
  number.

* ipl_confirm_ticks and av4_refract_ticks were locals in ipl_task, which
  spins on core 3 at SCHED_FIFO and must not divide. They are globals
  now, and the ns -> tick conversion is done by the setter on the CPU
  thread; ipl_task publishes cntfrq for it.

* The ET4000 frame budget was a stack local set once, so fps needed a
  restart. It is re-read every pass - one compare against a global - and
  the guest-follow branch still wins when it is on.

Two getenv() calls leave hot paths on the way: PISTORM_GEMDOS_DEBUG was
read on every GEMDOS trap and PISTORM_NET_DEBUG on every frame.

Also noted where it matters: PISTORM_BLIT_INSTANT never existed (a stale
comment in st_blitter.c promises it) - instant is blit_timed_ns == 0; and
the ST Box slice budget was the compile-time SLICE_CYC in guest cycles,
not a nanosecond cap, so the control sets cycles.
